Edward Norton was deeply affected by the  experience of making American History X  partly due to the film's dark subject  matter and partly because the production  was a very troubled one here's why the  actor was never quite the same after  making this film in 1996 Norton's  performance in primal fear established  him as a formidable talent but it was  his starring role in 1998's American  History X that truly showcased his  talents in the film Norton plays Derek  Vinyard a neo-nazi who abandons his  ideology after spending some time in  prison he then tries to prevent his  younger brother Danny played by Edward  Furlong from following in his footsteps  as Norton told GQ in 2019 the film is  like even the poster was iconic it was a  Shakespearean type of a story by design  it's rare that an actor has to step in  and edit the film they're starring in  but that's exactly what Edward Norton  ended up doing that's because the making  of American History X proved to be  almost as controversial as the film  itself and the film was plenty  controversial in the middle of the  editing process director Tony K had some  serious creative differences with the  production company New Line Cinema K had  previously worked as an advertising  director and his experience in that  industry reportedly affected his editing  decisions in fact David McKenna the  film's screenwriter told The Telegraph  that Kay literally destroyed the movie  it was a commercial in the hopes of  salvaging the film Norton stepped in and  began working with K but they were  constantly at odds with one another  however many of Norton's edits did wind  up in the final cut of the film it was a  new experience for Norton but the skill  came in handy later he ended up Co  editing the 2005 Neel Western down in  the valley a film he also starred in  after filming wrapped on American  History X director Tony K was very  bitter towards Edward Norton and he  wasn't shy about letting the media know  his thoughts  The Telegraph quotes him saying that  Norton is a narcissistic dilettante  obsessed with his image obsessed with  screen time I was going to take  Hollywood by storm and I  would have but for that buffoon keep in  mind K doesn't seem particularly modest  himself I'm a storyteller so you know  making a television commercial is he's  telling a story of souls thanks to all  the drama behind the scenes during the  filming of American History X Norton  earned a reputation for being difficult  to work with but the actor rejects that  characterization as he told The  Telegraph difficult is really a question  of semantics you can use that word or  you can say rigorous committed you can  put it other ways and I'll tell you a  very true thing none of what I would  call the genuine talents I've worked  with directors have ever called me  difficult in a 2014 interview with Dan  Rather Edward Norton confessed that  making American History X was really  hard it was it was a gorilla kind of  experience  Norton put in some serious hours at the  gym to convincingly play Derek as men's  workout reports the actor gained 30  pounds of muscle in just three months  the magazine quotes Norton as saying I  knew this guy was going to have to be  really physically fearsome and defined  by rage arming himself against his own  emotional pain in this body he's created  is the physical manifestation of that  after the film was released people  suddenly perceived Norton as bigger  stronger and more intimidating as he  said on the Tim Ferriss show in 2016  doing that film created the strangest  distortion of perception on me he went  on to say it's unbelievable the degree  to which that film and the magic of  camera and art in black-and-white  photography and all these things made a  lot of people think that I was a larger  and tougher person than I am  it sounds like Edward Norton has never  been too concerned about box office  returns he'd much rather participate in  films that make a real cultural impact  to hear him say it American History X is  a film that really managed to capture  the zeitgeist and he's proud to have  been a part of it in 2010 he told / film  a lot of films are more successful  financially than Fight Club or American  History X but those films meant more to  people and continued to to me if I can  make a couple of films that looking back  people say yeah that's what our  generation felt like at that moment  that's a good document then I'm content  in a 2016 interview with ve T Norton was  asked whether he thinks the issue of  racism has changed since making the film  in 1998 he responded to a degree we're  not often comfortable acknowledging this  this current of racism sits under the  surface  he added that anxiety is often the cause  of those feelings saying people quote  have anxieties and they look for a  target Derrick goes through quite a  dramatic personal transformation in  American history X and that journey is  one of the things that really excited  Edward Norton about the character as he  told Charlie Rose in 1998 he goes on to  explain that it's not even so much just  the one very extreme manifestation of  this guy but more the emotional distance  that he travels but Norton had to do  more than convincingly portray Derrick's  hatred he also had to create a nuanced  character who could learn how to become  a more open-minded person throughout the  course of the film ultimately Norton  interpreted Derek Vinyard as a  classically tragic figure let's look at  someone who ruins all of the potential  in his life because of rage Norton also  wanted audiences to see something of  themselves in vineyard despite the  extreme and extremely ugly nature of his  character in 1999 he told the Guardian  an all-too-common reaction to something  like racism is to hate the act so much  you dismissed the person but in this  film you're forced to confront the  complexity of the character and his  tragedy check out one of our newest  videos right here plus even more looper  videos about your favorite movies are  coming soon subscribe to our YouTube  channel and hit the bell so you don't  miss a single one  [Music]
 

